<h1>The Combine Numbers Behind the Backups Who <em>Balled Out</em></h1>
<div class="hero-meta">
<span class="hero-meta-item">📖 6 min read</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">💪 Squat &amp; Speed</span>
<span class="hero-meta-item">🏈 Preseason Finale 2026</span>
</div>
<p class="hero-intro">
    Kyle McCord threw four touchdowns to rally the Packers past the Cardinals, and Sam Ehlinger tossed three in a Broncos blowout — both on the same preseason-finale Friday night, both fighting for roster spots. Neither one is a combine standout. Here's what their testing profiles actually say, and why it barely matters.
  </p>
</div><div class="article-wrap">
<div class="content">
<div class="callout" style="background:var(--surface);border-color:var(--accent);">
<div class="callout-icon">⚡</div>
<div class="callout-text"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Kyle McCord went <strong>22-of-35 for 226 yards and 4 TDs</strong> in a 42-38 win over Arizona, and Sam Ehlinger threw multiple touchdowns in a 34-6 rout of Minnesota — but McCord <strong>skipped the 40-yard dash</strong> at his combine and Ehlinger ran a slow <strong>4.93</strong> at his. Neither is an athletic marvel by testing standards. Both delivered when it mattered most for their roster spots.</div>
</div>
<h2>A Wild Friday Night for Backup Quarterbacks</h2>
<p>Preseason finales rarely matter competitively, but they matter enormously for the players fighting to make a 53-man roster. Both the Packers and Cardinals rested their starters, handing Kyle McCord the entire game. The 2025 sixth-round pick, who spent last season on Philadelphia's practice squad, delivered: he went 22 of 35 for 226 yards, threw four touchdowns without a turnover, and rallied Green Bay from an 11-point deficit with two scores to Kisean Johnson in the final 3:30. "It's out of my control at this point," McCord said afterward. "I did everything that I could've to make a case for myself."</p>
<p>The same night, Broncos QB3 hopeful Sam Ehlinger threw multiple touchdown passes — including one to running back Cody Schrader — in a 34-6 win over Minnesota, making a real push to be Sean Payton's third quarterback behind Bo Nix and Jarrett Stidham. Meanwhile, in Baltimore, the Ravens' kicking competition took a step backward: Tyler Loop missed two field goals and was booed by the home crowd amid growing job-security questions.</p>
<h2>The Combine Profile Behind McCord's Big Night</h2>
<p>McCord's testing background is almost the opposite of what you'd expect from a quarterback who just threw four touchdowns under pressure. He measured in at 218 pounds at the 2025 combine but <strong>did not run the 40-yard dash</strong>, and scouting reports consistently flagged his arm strength as below top-tier, noting the ball would hang on throws 25-plus yards downfield. His athletic profile was described as below average for the position overall.</p>
