<a class="calc-cta-btn" href="/squat-strength-sprint-speed">Compare Squat & Speed →</a>
</div>
<h2>Why Testing Numbers Miss the Point at Quarterback</h2>
<p>Of every position on the field, quarterback is the one where combine testing correlates least with NFL outcomes. Arm strength, mobility, and size are all measurable — but none of them capture the two things that actually separate a roster-caliber backup from a cut player: decision-making speed and accuracy under duress. McCord's coach put it plainly: "He definitely has the demeanor, no doubt about it. He's in total control out there, and he's willing to stand in the pocket, deliver in the face of pressure," Packers coach Matt LaFleur said. That's not something a stopwatch or a vertical jump measures.</p>
<p>Ehlinger's career has followed the same pattern — a physical talent (mobility, toughness, a live arm) whose NFL survival has always hinged more on situational football IQ and locker-room value than raw testing numbers.</p>
<h2>What It Means for the 53-Man Cutdown</h2>
<p>Both performances landed at the worst possible time to be ignored — the NFL's roster cutdown deadline lands Sunday, Aug. 30 at 6 p.m. ET, when all 32 teams trim from 90 to 53 players. McCord's four-touchdown night gives Green Bay's front office a real decision to make on a fringe roster spot, and Ehlinger's showing strengthens his case to be kept as Denver's emergency QB3. Neither outcome was predicted by a stopwatch back in college — both were earned on the practice and game field, which is exactly the point.</p>
